---
<QuestionLayout
  title="How do I read the model output frame on a native Traverse host?"
  description="On traverse-runtime 0.17.1, ExactModelMediatedConnector::activated() keeps the connector so you can read output_ref after model.execute. activate_exact_model_runtime still consumes the host."
  canonical="https://traverse-framework.com/questions/how-do-i-read-the-model-output-frame-on-a-native-traverse-host.html"
  category="Runtime and architecture"
  relatedLinks={relatedLinks}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <p><strong>Short answer:</strong> on crates.io <code>traverse-runtime</code> 0.17.1, keep <code>ExactModelMediatedConnector</code> in an <code>Arc</code> and activate it with <code>activated()</code>. After the run, <code>with_host_mut</code> reads the bytes behind <code>output_ref</code>. <code>activate_exact_model_runtime</code> still takes the host by value, so that call does not leave you a handle.</p>

  <h2>The mediated path</h2>
  <p>This is the Spec 104 binding, where a guest calls <code>connector_invoke</code> and the host answers. The v0.17.1 changelog entry <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/issues/1785">#1785</a> (pull request <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/pull/1809">#1809</a>) adds <code>activated()</code> and <code>activated_version()</code>. The snippet below is the one in <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/blob/v0.17.1/docs/host-connector-command-dispatch.md">docs/host-connector-command-dispatch.md</a> at that tag. <code>declared_requirements</code> comes from the capability contract. <code>max_bytes</code> is the caller's ceiling.</p>
  <pre><code>{nativeSnippet}</code></pre>
  <p><code>activate_exact_model_runtime</code> is unchanged. It is still the function that takes the host. Use <code>activated()</code> when the caller has to read the frame afterward. <code>activated_version(version)</code> activates at an explicit connector version.</p>

  <h2>On the browser and Node host</h2>
  <p>npm <code>traverse-embedder-web@0.17.1</code> does not use <code>activated()</code>. <code>ExactModelBrowserHost</code> stays with the caller. A successful <code>execute</code> returns <code>output_ref</code>, and <code>host.io.readModelOutput</code> reads it. The ref is opaque. In that package the store mints ids of the form <code>output-</code> plus a counter. A missing ref throws <code>ExactModelError</code> code <code>unavailable</code>, message <code>output_ref missing or expired</code>. A frame longer than the ceiling you pass throws code <code>input_limit_exceeded</code>, message <code>output exceeds read ceiling</code>. The read does not delete the stored bytes. <code>input_ref</code> is the one that is single-use.</p>
  <pre><code>{webSnippet}</code></pre>
  <p>Which model produced the frame is <code>result.model_evidence</code>, not the byte length. That record is <a href="/questions/how-does-a-traverse-host-prove-which-model-it-ran.html">its own page</a>.</p>

  <h2>What this does not cover</h2>
  <p>The v0.17.1 dispatch doc says Swift, Android, and .NET activation APIs for this mediated binding are not shipped. Those embedders do ship <code>ExactModelHost</code> for direct execute. This page is the mediated frame read on <code>traverse-runtime</code> 0.17.1, plus the web <code>readModelOutput</code> call. No host is Certified under Spec 529.</p>
</QuestionLayout>

